{"attack": true, "case": "The target claims the preference for better-corroborated theories is 'non-inductive' because it is merely 'a report on current survival status.' But the claim simultaneously frames the preference as a 'pragmatic decision to act on the theory that has withstood the most severe tests.' A decision to ACT is inherently forward-looking: it commits future behavior on the basis of a theory's track record. To treat 'withstood the most severe tests' as the relevant criterion for which theory to act on is to assert that past survival is a reason bearing on future reliance. That is precisely the inferential structure of induction, regardless of whether one labels it a 'prediction.' The countercondition attempting to exclude 'more likely to be true in the future based on past performance' cannot rescue the claim: it surgically forbids the probabilistic phrasing while permitting the identical inference under the label 'pragmatic preference.' If the preference were truly ONLY a 'report on current survival status,' it would be a description, not a preference and not a basis for action — making the claim a category error. If it is genuinely a preference guiding action, it relies on the past-to-future relevance the countercondition tries to forbid. The target cannot coherently be both a mere report and a non-inductive pragmatic guide to action. Popper's own position is notoriously contested precisely on this point, so presenting it as cleanly 'non-inductive' overstates the defensible content.", "premise": "A pragmatic decision to act on a theory because it has withstood severe tests is forward-looking and uses past performance as the reason for future reliance, which is the inferential structure of induction.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "833a9dce23eab828e115cbaac87f3ae2bf49216d45498817ea9bf0dbe50042e9", "quote": "defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, pragmatic decision to act on the theory that has withstood the most severe tests, acknowledging it may still be false but is currently the 'fittest' survivor."}, {"block": "833a9dce23eab828e115cbaac87f3ae2bf49216d45498817ea9bf0dbe50042e9", "quote": "Preference is not a prediction of future success (induction) but a report on current survival status."}], "successor_question": "Can a forward-looking pragmatic rule for acting be grounded in a theory's past survival without any claim that past survival is evidentially relevant to future reliability?"}